     1	VALIDATED
     2	
     3	# What is proven
     4	
     5	Under the supplied `MPY` reference semantics, for every finite semantic list
     6	`FULL` satisfying `allInts(FULL)`, loading the exact translated
     7	`pairs_sum_to_zero` definition and calling it returns a Boolean equal to
     8	`anyInverse(FULL, FULL)`.
     9	
    10	`anyInverse(FULL, FULL)` is true exactly when some integer occurrence has a
    11	distinct-position additive inverse occurrence. For a nonzero `x`, the inverse
    12	must occur at least once. For zero, zero must occur at least twice. Thus the
    13	result is true exactly when two distinct list positions contain integers whose
    14	sum is zero.
    15	
    16	This is a partial-correctness reachability proof. It establishes the result
    17	whenever the call terminates; termination is not a separate liveness theorem.
    18	
    19	# Formal claim
    20	
    21	`spec.k` contains:
    22	
    23	- `SPEC.loop-invariant`: starting at the exact source-loop head with remaining
    24	  sequence `REM`, full input `FULL`, and current Boolean `FOUND`, fixed
    25	  execution finishes with
    26	  `FOUND orBool anyInverse(REM, FULL)`. The final, unobserved `x` binding is
    27	  existentially framed as a `Val`.
    28	- `SPEC.pairs-sum-to-zero`: starts at `#loadAll(Module(...))` containing the
    29	  exact `solution.mpy` function body followed by a wrapper assignment that
    30	  calls the loaded binding. The final `$result` is constrained to
    31	  `anyInverse(FULL, FULL)`.
    32	
    33	The precondition `allInts(FULL)` matches the prompt's list-of-integers domain.
    34	The observable final state is the returned Boolean, captured by the wrapper's
    35	`$result`. The wrapper itself only observes the return value.
    36	
    37	The loop proof discharges:
    38	
