   320	# Gate results
   321	
   322	## Gate A — PASS
   323	
   324	- A1: `spec.k` loads and calls the exact `solution.mpy` body. Program-defined
   325	  code, lookup, method binding, calls, loop execution, and return all run under
   326	  fixed semantics. The body mutation is rejected.
   327	- A2: the only operational bridges are pure value equations and touch no state.
   328	  The loop claim explicitly accounts for every modified local binding and
   329	  preserves other cells.
   330	- A3: fixed lookup selects the loaded closure and fixed attribute/call rules
   331	  select `list.count`. Arguments and branches execute in source order. Both
   332	  bridges have bridge-free universal connection claims over their full guarded
   333	  value domains and preserve context/control.
   334	- A4: every proof-local function has disjoint, exhaustive, terminating
   335	  equations. No false off-domain equation or result-bearing oracle is present.
   336	- A5: the empty-list precondition is realizable. The deliberately false result
   337	  is rejected, as are the source-body and both opposite-value mutations.
   338	
   339	## Gate B — PASS
   340	
   341	- Input domain: finite lists of mathematical integers, exactly the prompt's
   342	  stated domain. Non-integers and K `Bool` values are excluded.
   343	- Model adequacy: K `Int` is unbounded, matching Python integer arithmetic for
   344	  the used operations. List iteration, integer equality/negation, `count`,
   345	  function binding, and return are modeled by the supplied semantics.
   346	- Property adequacy: `hasInverse` encodes distinct positions by requiring two
   347	  zeros for `x == 0` and one inverse occurrence for nonzero `x`;
   348	  `anyInverse(FULL, FULL)` structurally expresses existence of such an
   349	  occurrence. This is the requested human-facing property, not merely an
   350	  execution trace summary.
   351	- Implementation alignment: concrete examples and exhaustive small-domain
   352	  differential tests agree with the contract.
   353	
   354	## Gate C — PASS
   355	
   356	- Every proof extension and dependency is inventoried above.
   357	- Positive proof, concrete execution, body/postcondition mutations, bridge
   358	  mutations, and differential evidence have existing artifacts, exact commands,
   359	  outputs, and exit statuses.
   360	- Formal results, finite evidence, trust assumptions, and excluded behavior are
   361	  separated below.
   362	
   363	# Trust boundary
   364	
   365	- Trusted input: the supplied read-only `reference-semantics/` definition as
   366	  the model of the supported Python subset.
   367	- Trusted machinery: K 7.1.293, its Haskell reachability backend, and LLVM
   368	  concrete backend.
   369	- No opaque float, sorting, digest, external, or result-bearing trusted
   370	  primitive is used by this program or proof.
   371	- The operational bridges are not trusted assumptions: both are connected to
   372	  fixed execution by bridge-free `#Top` claims.
   373	- The theorem is partial correctness; termination is outside its formal
   374	  conclusion. For finite lists the implementation plainly performs one finite
   375	  outer traversal and finite `count` traversals, but that is not a kprove
   376	  liveness result.
   377	
   378	# Empirically supported facts
   379	
   380	- LLVM execution passed nine concrete assertions under the supplied semantics.
   381	- CPython execution matched an independently written double-index oracle for
   382	  all 3,906 lists of lengths 0 through 5 over values `-2..2`.
   383	- These finite checks support implementation/intent and concrete-semantics
   384	  alignment. They are not used as substitutes for either universal K proof.
   385	
   386	# Excluded behavior
   387	
   388	- Inputs containing non-integers, including Python/K booleans.
   389	- Behavior outside constructs modeled by the supplied reference semantics.
   390	- Resource bounds and termination as formal liveness properties.
   391	- Mutation probes are deliberately false artifacts and are not part of the
   392	  positive theorem.

